The purpose of this pull request is to allow masonry and xilem to share the event loop with the rest of the application but still essentially own and manage the event loop. There is also a small hack to allow widgets to always repaint, but that isn't meant to be included in the final pull request (left it in for information purposes).

## Breakdown of changes:

### Masonry

* Masonry ApplicationHandler is extended to include all the winit callbacks.
* Masonry AppDriver is extended to include winit callbacks, but with an additional MasonryState interface for communication with masonry.
  - Some of the callbacks on AppDriver also determine whether an event is further processed by masonry. In particular, if window_event, device_event, or user_event return true then masonry will not process the event.
  - The added methods to the AppDriver trait all have default implementations which maintain current behavior.
* MainState in event_loop_runner now contains just app_driver and MasoneryState, which is now the home to all the other fields that used to be in MainState. This change is to deal with mutability issues (need to pass a mutable MasonryState to AppDriver methods). This moved a lot of code, but didn't really change very much.
* MasonryState, which is passed to AppDriver, only has a few public methods:
   - submit_window_event, submit_device_event, and submit_user_event.
   - these are used to submit winit events to masonry, typically not generated by event loop.
   - two applications for this are: 1)getting better control over the timing of rendering, and 2) journaling of events for playback (my game example will demonstrate both these features).

### Xilem

* new traits XilemToAppInterface and AppToXilemInterface are used to communicate between app and xilem.
* XilemToAppInterface forwards the winit callbacks via masonry to the application.
   - Like the callbacks on AppDriver,  window_event, device_event, and user_event callbacks can return true to indicate that events were handled by application.
   - The callbacks also receive an AppToXilemInterface in order to make calls to xilem.
      - These calls include submit_window_event, submit_device_event, and submit_user_event like the masonry version, and also get_state (possibly get_root in the future).
* When creating the xilem app, one can optionally use the with_app_interface method to add an app interface which implements the XilemToAppInterface trait. If you don't do this then behavior will be exactly as before.
   - ```  let app = Xilem::new(app_state, app_logic).with_app_interface(Box::new(AppInterface::new()));```

### Example

In the following example, the application hooks into resumed/suspended to change the control flow of the event loop, block redraw events coming from the event loop, and uses about_to_wait to update game world and push a redraw request to xilem.

```
type GameState = Arc<Mutex<GameWorld>>;

struct AppInterface {}

impl XilemToAppInterface<GameState> for AppInterface {
    fn resumed(&self, event_loop: &winit::event_loop::ActiveEventLoop, _: &mut dyn AppToXilemInterface<GameState>, _: &mut masonry::event_loop_runner::MasonryState<'_>) {
        event_loop.set_control_flow(winit::event_loop::ControlFlow::Poll);
    }

    fn suspended(&self, event_loop: &winit::event_loop::ActiveEventLoop, _: &mut dyn AppToXilemInterface<GameState>, _: &mut masonry::event_loop_runner::MasonryState<'_>) {
        event_loop.set_control_flow(winit::event_loop::ControlFlow::Wait);
    }

    fn window_event(&self, _event_loop: &winit::event_loop::ActiveEventLoop, _window_id: winit::window::WindowId, event: &winit::event::WindowEvent, xilem_interface: &mut dyn AppToXilemInterface<GameState>, masonry_state: &mut masonry::event_loop_runner::MasonryState<'_>) -> bool { 
        if *event == winit::event::WindowEvent::RedrawRequested {
            // we block redraw requests because we draw during about_to_wait
            true
        }
        else {
            false
        }
     }
        
    fn about_to_wait(&self, event_loop: &winit::event_loop::ActiveEventLoop, xilem_interface: &mut dyn AppToXilemInterface<GameState>, masonry_state: &mut masonry::event_loop_runner::MasonryState<'_>) {
        // this is safe because xilem and masonry don't track window id, but we should instead remember our window id and use that
        let fake_window_id = unsafe { winit::window::WindowId::dummy() };

        let game_state = xilem_interface.get_state();
        game_state.lock().unwrap().update();

        xilem_interface.submit_window_event(event_loop, masonry_state, fake_window_id, winit::event::WindowEvent::RedrawRequested);
    }
}

fn main() -> Result<(), EventLoopError> {
    let game_state = GameState::new(Mutex::new(create_game_world()));

    let app = Xilem::new(game_state, app_logic).with_app_interface(Box::new(AppInterface{}));
    app.run_windowed(EventLoop::with_user_event(), "test".into())?;
    Ok(())
}
```

Note that GameState is an Arc<Mutex<GameWorld>> because it needs to be shared between xilem app, widget view, and widget. If there is a better way to do that let me know.

These changes replace convert the unwrapped phase to range change and replace the current `unwrapped_phase` layer with `range_change` layer.

The test product header is as follows:

```
float range_change(y, x) ;
	range_change:_FillValue = NaNf ;
	string range_change:units = "meters" ;
	string range_change:long_name = "Range change" ;
	string range_change:grid_mapping = "spatial_ref" ;
```
